Z||000026||EdsB52||08-30-2001||04:00 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Tonker:<BR>[B][/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>"That's because they couldn't be bothered to go research an accurate description of Communism. What they've actually described is Leninism or Stalinism."<P>Was written in response to me stating this:<P>"Webster’s defines communism as, "a system of social organization in which all economic and social activity is controlled by a totalitarian state."<P>I think Webster's has been accepted as a standard in regard to the English language. To refute Webster's, you better have a PhD or two laying around and present a few reputable author’s rendition that successfully, unanimously disagrees with what Webster's writes. Until then, I think we can accept the abbreviated version of Webster. <P>If you want further reinforcement of what Webster's states, read what Houghton-Mifflin's American Heritage dictionary writes.<P>"A system of government in which the state plans and controls the economy and a single, often authoritarian party holds power, claiming to make progress toward a higher social order in which all goods are equally shared by the people."<P>It goes on to state:<P>"The Marxist-Leninist version of Communist doctrine that advocates the overthrow of capitalism by the revolution of the proletariat." <P>The proletariat is the common workingman for all who don’t know (not trying to condescend). So it appears to me that the dictionaries’ have it right; to what you are referring is the forceful opposition to capitalism or liberalism.<P>"I agree, though, that the media focuses on the symptoms of road-rage rather than the causes. Then again when someone cuts me off, I don't try to kill them, I flip them the bird and curse at them for a while."<P>Agreed. Just like the rate of impeding traffic tickets to speeding tickets is probably 1/1,000,000. Like I said, communistic. If it were capitalistic, the rules would be every man or woman for him or herself. There would be no tolerance for people that couldn’t keep up and they would yank licenses for impeding traffic. Just as most systems are quasi, so is American capitalism.||||reg||1
Z||000027||EdsB52||08-30-2001||07:31 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by JSocha:<BR><B> WOW!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif">   I can not believe this!  I to actually agree with Ed on this.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>"Lets throw something else into the discussion for support on Eds assertion, that regarding Lidar and the good old fashioned Radar."<P>Is Lidar an acronym for laser? In Arizona, there are 4 ways for the cops to report a speeding ticket.<BR>1) Pace<BR>2) Radar (X, K, Ka)<BR>3) Laser<BR>4) VASCAR (RxT=D)<P>"American society is always taken in by the perpetual cons and scam artists that are out there.  Our government supports and assists in stopping these individuals from taking money unnecessarily from unsuspecting people.<P>Yet, at the same time, in the same breath, our government officials are perpetuating a scam on to us through the issuance of "speeding" tickets."<P>Our government presents the facade of trying to overthrow con artists, but in reality doesn't mind them as long as they get a cut and don't embarrass anyone important. Speeding tickets are just another scam in the long list of other scams, in my opinion.<P>"Ever been pulled over and issued a speeding ticket when you in fact new 100% you were not speeding?  Ever tried to contest it?  No win situation unless you have an attorney worth his weight and then some in gold helping you."<P>No comment on the first question, however I've received many tickets where the cop obviously 'guesstimated' my speed. I might have my top of the line radar detector on the windshield, and have a cop tell me he got me on K-band radar. You can't contest the ticket based on that defense, but I know in my mind that he lied. I contest 95% of them. Not always true, but the concept of 'preponderance of evidence' or 'beyond a reasonable doubt' are not present in kangaroo courts. Kangaroo courts are the lower courts where traffic tickets are disposed. In Arizona, lower court (JP's and Municipal courts) judges aren't required to be members of the State Bar or to even have formal training other than what the city/county gives them. For example, a local court around here has a judge that was a high-school teacher that was appointed by the city council and has presided there for years. But I ultimately agree with you in that you are behind the eight ball before you walk in. I've appealed 4 of them, one to the State Supreme Court, and have won two.<P>"Lidar and Radar do not have the following ability, to download reports from their system of the transactions during the respective day including date, time and speed stamps.  They do not have a camera/video feature in most cases to show that you were in fact the vehicle being targeted, cloced and subsequently followed."<P>Again, the standard of proof is well below even a preponderance in the lower courts, as you well illustrate.<P>"When you go to court, the officer only has to state that he is trainied in the system and that it was calibrated before and after his shift, with no confirmational support as far as a calibration report or witnessed by another individual stating that it was calibrated prior to and at the end of a respective shift.  They only have to indicate that they were in a respective area, noted your vehicle was traveling faster then the rest, indicate the speed, distance and the amount of time they tracked you prior to pulling you over with really no further evidence."<P>True, and most of the judges are totally ignorant as to how these systems work; they just rely on the 'good word' of the officer as you've alluded.<P>"You can take the speed, distance (at which he clocked you to the time you passed his position or over the entire distance before you pulled over where the time will be relavent) and the amount of time in your own defense that the officer declared within his testimony to refute his argument, only to have the officers ticket upheld by the court."<P>Correct, because you are likely talking to a babbling, politically motivated idiot. And the cop probably isn't as bright.<P>"Also, in most states, LIDAR and RADAR are not under "Judicial Notice" ((or is it Review) Ed, please help me out if I got that term wrong).  Simply stated, this means that the state would have to bring in scientists, researches and everyone else to provide testimony that LIDAR or RADAR works as the manufacturer of these units claim."<P>Judicial notice is a courtroom tool used to establish an obvious fact. I used it once when I proved the cop broke the law by turning off his headlight (motorcycle) to attempt to sneak up on me. Even though it was fairly bright out and he didn't sneak up on me, I provided a copy of the newspaper to establish, through judicial notice that dawn was before the time in which he turned off his headlights. This was off topic, but since cops and judges 69 each other, the audiotape somehow became unintelligible (for the sake of the cop), I petitioned for a Trial De Novo (new trial), and the case was transferred to the Superior court where the cop and the state didn't show. You can win these cases; you just have to want to. So anyway, judicial notice might be used ask the court to accept the fact that August 31, 2001 is on a Friday.<P>Judicial review is more dynamic. It generally refers to a court's power to review the actions of other branches of government. A higher court can also review the actions or procedures of a lower court under the doctrine of judicial review. I think that's to what you are referring. When it comes to civil traffic tickets, the higher courts are hard-pressed to interfere. I wonder how many people in a position to review these procedures have tickets on their records or are harassed the cops?<P>"However, in the end, no matter how you plead your case, the testimony and support you can provide, the attorney you get to fight it, the judge will always side with the state."<P>I wouldn't say always, but generally yes. I win at least half my tickets in court. The ones that I lose, I appeal and can keep them off my record for at least a year, while costing the State a couple thousand dollars to secure a civil conviction.<P>"In it, he stated that any officer can issue a ticket, regardless if it is correct or not and have it upheld by the court, even if it was issued falsly in the first place to either enhance a future performance review, meet an objective quota to cut down on speeding noted in a particular area or to bring more income into the state.  Now doesn't that constitue FRAUD?  If not a state perpetuated and enforced SCAM through the use of legal means to the unsuspecting public?"<P>I just don't think the public is unsuspecting. I think people realize how corrupt most cops and judges are, but just don't know what to do about it. Yes, it definitely is fraud. Hints on how you can fight a ticket when you are in the courtroom are to first establish the proximity of the cop in every aspect of his testimony. If he paced you for 3 miles, ask him how far behind/ahead of you he was. Ask what angle (perspective) he was to your vehicle. Ask the officer how fast he had to drive to catch you. That works especially well if he claims he was stopped along side the road using radar or laser, clocked you at 65 in a 40, and then had accelerate to catch you. You ask him where he was situated when he radared you, and where he stopped you. The shorter the distance the better. You can establish, with our without his testimony, that he had to drive 80+ MPH to catch you. Of course he will deny having driven that fast and you can assert you weren't driving that fast either. Either way, make him establish his proximity first or he'll lie.||||reg||1
Z||000028||EdsB52||08-30-2001||07:49 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Cheever3000:<BR><B>[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>"First, there [b]is</B> an element of true rage which is definitely a problem on our streets & highways, like that nut with the gun."<P>True, but if a person instigates another, who is liable? Impeding traffic constitutes instigation to me.<P>"Second, the show <B>did</B> (briefly) mention that everybody wants to be first in line no matter what, and common courtesy is harder to find."<P>Ya, but when a person runs to the front of the line only to hold everyone up, then that person is not being courtoeus.<P>"But they made the assumption that anger is wrong.  All anger is <B>NOT</B> wrong!  I don't even think I need to explain that."<P>True.<P>"And here's the thing my wife and I disagreed on: Yes, it was <I>legal</I> for them to drive only 65 mph in the fast lane.  Her argument is anybody wanting to go faster has no right to be upset at them, and can simply go around them.  ~sigh~ (I tried to reason with her, guys.)  My answer is, first, why obey one rule but disobey the one that says if you're not passing someone then stay to the right?  Geez, stay out of people's way even if they are speeding."<P>I couldn't agree with you more. But check your state law; I doubt it's against the law to drive 65mph in a 65 zone in the left lane, even if people are on your ass. The only impeding statute I know of in Arizona is driving with 5 people on your ass without pulling over. I think that pertains to single lane roads. I've never heard of a ticket being issued for that. When I was a kid we would drive through Oregon. I remember seeing road signs that read, "slower traffic to the right or a citation would be issued," or something to that effect. Now I don't see those. I think they came down with the 70mph laws in the early/mid 70's.<P>" Second, what's right about <I>them</I>(those who drive slow in the fast lane) being inconsiderate to someone wanting to go faster (no matter what their reason for it is)?  What's right about causing accidents by interrupting the flow of traffic and <I>making</I> people weave in & out to go around you?  You can be <B>legal</B>, but still be <B>wrong</B>. And it can be deadly.  That shows a lot of brains, and makes you a great citizen, huh?"<P>Excellent points. If someone is going to go "fast," and another person insists on driving the limit while impeding traffic, that second person is at least as culpable in my eyes, but unfortunately the courts don't see it that way. Legal, but wrong; that concept isn't limited to traffic.||||reg||1
Z||000029||EdsB52||08-30-2001||07:52 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 84Bill:<BR><B>BTW<P>PA has enacted a new law 2 months ago whereby you can be fined for driving in the left lane if the right lane is clear for you to move over.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Ya, how many citations will be issued? ZERO.||||reg||1
Z||000030||EdsB52||08-30-2001||07:59 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Cheever3000:<BR><B>That's great, but a cop has to see it, right? You can't just turn someone in.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Honestly, let's not rely on the government for enforcement any more than is necessary. In Arizona, we now have red light cameras on virtually every corner. We've had photo radar vans for sometime. The State Supreme Court has ruled that regular mail is not suitable service for mailing these tickets, so you just don't respond and you're alright, right? Not anymore. Now the city/State/county is contracting private process server companies to serve these tickets. Not to mention the assholes in the vans are private contracted citizens, so we the people are punking each other out for 5 or 10 bucks and then turning around and cursing the government for being over-controlling. I hate to see people telling on each other for kicks. If someone is personally offending you, that's different.||||reg||1
Z||000031||Butter||08-30-2001||01:09 PM||||Cheever3000<BR>I very much agree about the a$$hole drivers being the cause of most of the problems of road rage.<BR>To me it's like the supidity of gun control.  You just never hear of a gun killing anyone or anything it's the bullet that does the damage.  Amunition control!<BR>A$$hole divers are the amunition for road rage.  Respect for others would go a long way in these situations.  <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000032||Cheever3000||08-30-2001||01:16 PM||||I'm with you there, B52.  I think.  I meant to imply that it would be nice to turn someone in for hindering the flow of traffic.  Back when there weren't so many cars on the road it wasn't such an issue.  You could still get where you wanted to go.  And that was before the big wide highways we have in some places today (Houston).  I mean, we got all those lanes, and people still go to the left one and slow down everybody.  And the problem is not just being slowed, it's the unpredictable weaving in & out that everybody has to do in order to get around the slow ones.  That is so unsafe.  That's why I want to be able to report such a driver.<P>I'd like to be able to report drivers who litter.  I once saw someone throw an entire fast food bag of trash on the highway right in front of me, and a state trooper was just over the next ridge.  So I stopped to tell him and give him the license number, but he said he couldn't do anything because HE didn't see it.<P>I hate litterers.<BR><P>------------------<BR><I>Ranger Chuck</I><BR><A HREF="http://home.houston.rr.com/fieropartsrelay" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Parts Relay</A> administrator||||reg||1
